.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.env
permissions.json
audit_log.jsonl
privacy_preferences.json
users.json
sessions.json
user_data/
.cli_session
.gui_session
.pytest_cache/
financial_intelligence.db
financial_intelligence.db-wal
financial_intelligence.db-shm

# The Gateway's own store: sessions and the Super User's conversation transcript.
# Separate from the FI database by design (see gateway/store.py), and no more
# versionable - it is live local state, and its session rows are credentials.
gateway.db
gateway.db-wal
gateway.db-shm
# Simulation run output: one directory per run, each holding its own database
# and log. Reproducible from the scenario plus the manifest's recorded seed and
# code version, so the runs themselves are not worth versioning.
simulation/runs/
# Backup sets (backend/continuity.py): copies of the live stores above, and
# exactly as sensitive - gateway.db session rows and sessions.json are
# credentials in the backup too.
backups/
# The secondary-destination encryption key (backend/continuity.py's
# ensure_backup_key): Tier-0 recovery material. A copy belongs OFF this
# machine; no copy ever belongs in the repository.
backup.key
# The model-spend ledger (app/model_budget.py): live local state, one row per
# UTC day, shared by every process that calls the model.
model_spend.db
# The model performance registry (app/model_performance.py, TQ-54): measured
# outcomes per model per task category, machine-written after every task. It is
# deliberately not committed - that is the whole reason it is a database rather
# than a section of docs/model_registry.yaml, which holds the hand-authored half.
model_performance.db
model_performance.db-wal
model_performance.db-shm
simulation/runs/

# Demo run summaries. Like simulation/runs, these are the output of a run
# rather than part of the system: reproducible from the run directories they
# point at, and different on every machine.
demo_summary.json

# Backups of .env hold the API key and the password hash. Never tracked.
.env.backup*
.env.*.bak

# The machines this deployment is allowed to diagnose (gateway/remote.py): their
# names, tailnet addresses, ports and the local paths of files their agents
# write. Every one of those is a property of one installation rather than of the
# code, which is the whole reason the module reads them from a file instead of
# containing them. remote-targets.example.json IS tracked and documents the
# shape; this is the live one.
config/remote-targets.json

# The model call log (app/model_calls.py, Task 01 Deliverable A), the retry
# queue and the capability-gap log. Live local state, one line per model call,
# rotated daily and kept 30 days. Not versionable for the same reason
# model_spend.db is not: it is what this machine did, not what the code is.
# MODEL_CALL_LOG_DIR moves the whole directory; the suite points it at a
# temporary one (tests/conftest.py).
logs/

# Self-diagnosis and skills-audit reports (app/self_diagnosis.py,
# app/capability_gaps.py). Generated from logs/ on a schedule, and regenerable
# from it - committing them would version one machine's night.
reports/
